community has begun to recognize that effective global action requires meaningful stakeholder involvement in international policymaking and implementation (Wapner, 2000). NGO involvement in global environmental governance can take a variety of forms (Esty, 1998, 2002; Charnovitz, 1997):
•
Expert advice and analysis. NGOs can facilitate negotiations by giv ing politicians access to competing ideas from outside the normal bureaucratic channels;
•
Intellectual competition to governments. NGOs often have much
better analytical and technical skills and capacity to respond more
